# docs/citations.assert — machine-checked citation content contracts (V13, TASK-379).
#
# Why this file exists: `docs/**` cites evidence as `file:line`, and check-docs used to
# verify only that the line EXISTS. A line number drifts on every unrelated edit above
# it, so a citation could point 100 lines away from its evidence and the gate stayed
# green. Sampling 20 citations found 7 clear drifts (about 35%) and check-docs reported
# 2 of them. This registry closes that hole for a whitelist of high-value citations
# instead of hashing every citation: hashing high-churn files would produce noise and
# rubber-stamping, and the `audit-citations` heuristic is not accurate enough to block.
#
# Format: TAB-separated, one contract per line.
#   file<TAB>line<TAB>contains<TAB>reason<TAB>cited_at
#     file      repository-relative path (no leading slash, no `..`)
#     line      the single cited line, 1-based
#     contains  literal substring that line must contain
#     reason    REQUIRED: why this pointer matters (an entry without one is an ERROR)
#     cited_at  REQUIRED here: where the document writes the citation. Two shapes:
#                 `<doc>:<line>`   absolute line number (legacy, still supported)
#                 `<doc>@<anchor>` unique literal substring of ONE line in `<doc>`
#               The document line must still cite the same file at a line covering
#               `line`; otherwise the registry and the prose have diverged and the gate
#               fails. This is the second direction of the check.
#
# Which shape to use: prefer the ANCHOR shape for documents that GROW. Inserting a
# line above the citation shifts every absolute line below it — that is exactly how
# PR #461 first went red, because docs/production-deploy.md appends a ~120-line
# section per deployment and the citations after the append point drifted with it.
# check-docs resolves an anchor with `grep -nF` at run time, so growth above it is
# harmless. The anchor must match EXACTLY ONE line: zero matches ("anchor not found")
# and several matches ("anchor is not unique") are both failures — never the first
# match, which would silently re-point the contract at unrelated text. Keep the line
# number form for stable files and for a citation line that has no unique literal.
# Every anchor in this file must exist verbatim on the cited_at line: editing that
# sentence now fails the gate instead of drifting silently.
# Comment lines start with `#`. Blank lines are ignored.
#
# Both directions are checked because either half can be edited alone:
#   - forward: the declared file exists, `line` is inside it, and that line contains
#     `contains`;
#   - reverse: the document at `cited_at` still writes a citation to that file covering
#     that line.
# The reverse check is line-local on purpose: it requires the cited_at line to name the
# file and to carry a `:N`/`:N-M`/`:N,M` citation covering the line. It does not
# re-resolve which span belongs to which file when a line cites several; that
# attribution is check-docs' check 6, and this layer is a consistency guard, not a
# security boundary.
# Scope is deliberately a whitelist. Full content hashing is a non-goal: the cited
# files change often, so it would either fail constantly or be exempted wholesale.
#
# TASK-379 fixed the measured drifts and two edge cases in the documents; the first
# entries below are those corrected citations, so the fixes cannot silently rot back.
# The rest seed the high-frequency, high-churn files: cmd/orchestrator/main.go,
# deploy/dev/dev.sh, cmd/auth/main.go, internal/orchestrator/service.go and
# internal/store/store.go.

